{
  "id": "grammar.a1.119.a",
  "title": "Spicy, salty, sour",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "What does it taste like?",
      "content": "To say what a dish is like, put the dish and the word together with no verb, as in *A leves meleg.* Three new taste words: *csípős* (spicy), *sós* (salty) and *savanyú* (sour). You know *nagyon* (very). *Túl* means \"too\" and is not a compliment: *nagyon finom* is good, *túl csípős* is too much."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Taste words",
      "rows": [
        [
          "A leves csípős.",
          "The soup is spicy."
        ],
        [
          "A hal sós.",
          "The fish is salty."
        ],
        [
          "A saláta savanyú.",
          "The salad is sour."
        ],
        [
          "A tészta túl csípős.",
          "The pasta is too spicy."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "Examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "A leves csípős.",
          "english": "The soup is spicy."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "A hal nagyon sós.",
          "english": "The fish is very salty."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "A saláta savanyú.",
          "english": "The salad is sour."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "A tészta túl csípős.",
          "english": "The pasta is too spicy."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "A torta nem savanyú.",
          "english": "The cake is not sour."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "There is no *van* in these sentences: *A leves csípős*, not *A leves van csípős*. Put *nagyon* or *túl* straight before the word."
    }
  ]
}
